<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Iron Arm</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">?–<a href="/years/1046">1046</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as William of Hauteville</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Iron Arm was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tancred-prince-of-galilee">Tancred of Hauteville</a> and the first leader of the Normans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>. Having fought for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-maniakes">George Maniakes</a>, where by tradition his single combat with the emir of Syracuse earned him his nickname, he was elected count by the Norman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melfi">Melfi</a> in 1042. He died in 1046 and was succeeded by his brother Drogo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-iron-arm.jpg" alt="William Iron Arm" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giogo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_cath%C3%A9drale_Coutances_Guillaume_Bras-de-fer.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
